{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 740,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The moment you started observing reality,\nyou hopelessly polluted any conclusions you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6170,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might make about it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7900,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The anthropic principle guarantees that you\nare NOT seeing the universe in its most typical state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14100,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But used correctly, this highly controversial\nidea can be extremely powerful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19820,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, how do you correctly use the anthropic\nprinciple?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29360,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1543 our perspective of the cosmos was\nradically disrupted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34190,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nicholaus Copernicus presented his model of\nthe solar system which demoted the Earth from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39230,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its position at the center of the universe\ninto just one of several planets orbiting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44100,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As our astronomy improved, we realized that\nour sun is a typical example out of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50840,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "100s of billions of stars in the Milky Way, and that\nthe Milky Way is an ordinary galaxy among"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56240,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "100s of billions of galaxies in the observable universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60600,
    "dDurationMs": 6769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The notion that we occupy neither a central\nnor privileged position in the universe is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67369,
    "dDurationMs": 4151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the Copernican principle after the\nguy who started it all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71520,
    "dDurationMs": 5529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s extremely important - it allows us\nto study the distant universe confident that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77049,
    "dDurationMs": 3901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its laws of physics are the same as we experience\non Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80950,
    "dDurationMs": 5309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It allows us to understand the origin of the\nEarth and the Milky Way by studying the ancient"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86259,
    "dDurationMs": 2311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light of distant galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88570,
    "dDurationMs": 5329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Copernican principle became such a powerful\ntool that it took centuries for us to realize"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93899,
    "dDurationMs": 4451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it’s wrong - or at least flawed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98350,
    "dDurationMs": 6689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It states that we don’t occupy a central,\nprivileged position, but that’s only half true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105039,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth certainly isn’t central, but it IS\nprivileged, and not at all a typical environment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112159,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nor, perhaps, is our entire universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115819,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our planet and our universe must have at least\none non-typical quality - they must have been"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122579,
    "dDurationMs": 6201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to produce us - to give rise to living\ncreatures that can observe it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128780,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the anthropic principle, and it seems\nto contradict the sacred Copernican principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133980,
    "dDurationMs": 6390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the last two episodes we talked about certain\nobservations of the uniqueness both of our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140370,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet and of the universe, and how these feed\ninto two versions of the anthropic principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145820,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to bring these ideas\ntogether with the Copernican principle to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150390,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see just how powerful - and how misleading\n- the anthropic principle can be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155900,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to the original definitions by Brandon\nCarter, the weak anthropic principle states"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161450,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we must live in a place and time in the\nuniverse capable of supporting observers - in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167450,
    "dDurationMs": 6950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our case, a habitable biosphere, and the strong\nanthropic principle, which states that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174400,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe itself must have the conditions necessary\nfor producing environments that, in turn,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180450,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce observers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181850,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the fundamental constants and initial\nconditions of the universe must be just right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186840,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to allow nice habitable planets to\nform."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190610,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s just call it the anthropic principle:\nwe necessarily observe from an environment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197850,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capable of producing observers; be that environment\na planet within a universe or a universe within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204370,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206330,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By itself, this statement is uncontroversial\n- some would even say tautological."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211710,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But things get interesting when you add the\nfact that our universe seems to have fundamental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216040,
    "dDurationMs": 6930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constants and initial conditions that seem\nextremely fine-tuned for the eventual formation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222970,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224130,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The anthropic principle permits a new explanation\nfor this fine-tuning besides blind luck or design:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230840,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if there are enough universes or enough\nregions within this universe, and their properties"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235770,
    "dDurationMs": 7690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can vary, then even if the vast majority are\nlifeless, life-friendly universes can still"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243460,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "show up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244460,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we shouldn’t be surprised that we find\nourselves in one, even though Earth and a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249200,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life supporting universe might be quite atypical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253350,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This use of the anthropic principle is highly\ncontentious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257250,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many scientists find it extremely unsatisfying,\nand lazy, and unscientific."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261960,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, it doesn’t tell us WHY the fundamental\nconstants take on the values that they do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265860,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- or why they may vary between universes. Or even why their should be multiple universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271220,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of the bad rap of the anthropic principle\ncomes from a distortion of the idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277120,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the 1986 book the Anthropic Cosmological\nPrinciple, John Barrow and Frank Tipler misinterpret"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283300,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strong anthropic principle to mean that\nthe evolution of observers somehow had some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290090,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causal influence on the initial formation\nof the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this commits the same flaw in reasoning that\nthe anthropic principle may solve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300289,
    "dDurationMs": 7111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The principle is NOT causal - it just tells\nus to account for an observer selection bias"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307400,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when interpreting the nature of our environment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310200,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So our finely-tuned part of the greater cosmos\ndoesn’t have to have been custom built for us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317360,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other common misuse is to assume that the anthropic principle allows for any degree of extreme"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322980,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fine tuning of the properties of our own environment,\nregardless of the global distribution of properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329300,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s an example, originally laid out by\nRoger Penrose."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333530,
    "dDurationMs": 7159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe’s most finely-tuned parameter\nis its unthinkably low initial entropy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340689,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The incredible density at the Big Bang was\na highly ordered state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345260,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All particles in the observable universe were\npacked together in a subatomic-sized dot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350640,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everything interesting that's happened since\n- from the formation of stars and galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354380,
    "dDurationMs": 5349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the evolution of life - has been powered\nby the slow increase in entropy from that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359729,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "initial state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361569,
    "dDurationMs": 5541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe will spend the vast majority\nof its perhaps-infinite life in a state of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367110,
    "dDurationMs": 7570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extreme disorder and high entropy - iron stars,\nblack holes, and a mist of cold elementary particles,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374680,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not very hospitable to life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376949,
    "dDurationMs": 5421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second law of thermodynamics tells us\nthat entropy can only increase, which means"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382370,
    "dDurationMs": 6091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extreme high entropy states must be the norm\n- in the full timeline of our universe, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388461,
    "dDurationMs": 4488,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably also across the multiverse, if it exists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392949,
    "dDurationMs": 5791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We certainly don’t observe the universe\nin a typical, observer-hostile state, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398740,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so it’s tempting to use the anthropic principle\nhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402260,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Low-entropy regions should be vastly less\ncommon than high-entropy regions, but if they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408960,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist we’re gonna find ourselves in one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411710,
    "dDurationMs": 1349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But guess what?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413059,
    "dDurationMs": 3991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That statement is wrong. Or at least incomplete."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417050,
    "dDurationMs": 6209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The anthropic principle appears to fail here, \n- but to understand why we need to go all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423259,
    "dDurationMs": 2611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way back to the Copernican principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425870,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we need to bring these two seemingly\nconflicting principles together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430580,
    "dDurationMs": 4559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in doing so we’ll end up with a much\nmore powerful version of the anthropic principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435139,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- one that will even make testable predictions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439139,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the Copernican principle, we are most likely\nto observe a very typical environment - this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444219,
    "dDurationMs": 1771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is just a statement of probability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445990,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are more typical environments than non-typical ones, so pick a random environment and its probably typical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452060,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But anthropic principle tells us we must account\nfor our status as observers when we interpret"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457560,
    "dDurationMs": 4699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our environment - including the probability\nof being in it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462259,
    "dDurationMs": 7421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s formulate a refined anthropic principle:\nwe should find ourselves in a typical region"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469680,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the cosmos that is consistent with us being\nobservers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by cosmos I mean the sum total of reality,\nbe it universe or multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478800,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That allows us to be in a rare, observer-friendly\nenvironment, but tells us that we should be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484530,
    "dDurationMs": 2879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the most typical of such environments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487409,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our perspective on the cosmos might be a rare\none, but it should be no more rare than is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492559,
    "dDurationMs": 3221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "necessary to explain our existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495780,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This refined anthropic principle gets really interesting when applied to our low entropy big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501680,
    "dDurationMs": 7540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If low-entropy regions happened just by chance fluctuations from a high entropy state,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509220,
    "dDurationMs": 4759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the lower the entropy the less probability of that region forming."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513979,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example a fluctuation the size of a galaxy\nis insanely more likely than one the size"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519039,
    "dDurationMs": 1391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our observable universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520430,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s much easier to produce 100s of billions\nof galaxy-sized fluctuations than a single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525060,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuation of 100s of billions of galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528790,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You surely don’t need more than one galaxy\nto spawn a life-bearing planet - so there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534140,
    "dDurationMs": 4999,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be many more observers in small entropy\nfluctuations than in large ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539139,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our refined anthropic principle appears to\nfail here - but actually it doesn’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543779,
    "dDurationMs": 4641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve just witnessed the potential power\nof this principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548420,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Under the assumption that our universe resulted\nfrom a simple random fluctuation in an otherwise"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553960,
    "dDurationMs": 6439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high-entropy cosmos, the anthropic principle\npredicts that we should be in the smallest such"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560399,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuation that could produce us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563089,
    "dDurationMs": 5631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Apparently we aren’t, and so we can probably\nrule out a simple random entropy fluctuation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568720,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a sufficient explanation for our big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572370,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or at the very least we need extra physics:\nfor example, we need the right initial density fluctuations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578680,
    "dDurationMs": 7540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to evolve and expand for the right\namount of time, so that life may always be in big universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586220,
    "dDurationMs": 5109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we then assume that the starting conditions\nfor our universe were typical, that can tell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591329,
    "dDurationMs": 3921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us something about the physics of how universes\nare born."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595250,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the anthropic principle to be useful and\nnot misleading, we need to be careful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Philosopher Nick Bostrom has made valiant\nattempts to clean up what he calls “anthropic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605920,
    "dDurationMs": 2899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reasoning” in his book Anthropic Bias."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608819,
    "dDurationMs": 4871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In it he defines, although doesn’t invent,\nthe self-sampling assumption, which states"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613690,
    "dDurationMs": 5399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that “All other things equal, an observer\nshould reason as if they are randomly selected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619089,
    "dDurationMs": 7091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the set of all actually existent observers\n(past, present and future) in their reference class.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626180,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If there are a bajillion observers in the\nentire cosmos, you should consider yourself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631300,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "randomly selected from them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633340,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means you’re most likely a common type\nof observer, and in a common environment in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638870,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which observers can exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641430,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The anthropic principle and the self-sampling\nassumption encourage Bayesian thinking."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647940,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We should take into account what we already know\n- our “priors” - before assessing any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654170,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probabilities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655720,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The prior is that we are an observer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659370,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But proper Bayesian thinking requires careful\ndefinition of priors - for example, Bostrom"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665390,
    "dDurationMs": 3309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talks about “observers in their reference\nclass”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668699,
    "dDurationMs": 3431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how do we know what our reference class is?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672130,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Carbon-based sentient life?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674250,
    "dDurationMs": 1710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All conscious entities?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675960,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anything capable of thinking about the anthropic\nprinciple?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679620,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even with very careful definitions, anthropic\nreasoning is difficult to use well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685170,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s evident in the various bizarre\npredictions it can make - from Boltzmann brains,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690100,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which we covered, to the doomsday argument\n- the idea that the self-sampling assumption"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695660,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predicts that the end of the world is nigh\n- which we’ll cover soon - hopefully soon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700870,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701870,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The anthropic principle in its proper form\nis without question an important thing to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706230,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take into account whenever we observe the\nuniverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709480,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s one possible explanation for why our\nplanet and our universe appear to fit us so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715100,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well, even if they weren’t intentionally\nmade for us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719440,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Douglass Adams put it best: “If you imagine\na puddle waking up one morning and thinking,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725720,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "'This is an interesting world I find myself\nin — an interesting hole I find myself in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730910,
    "dDurationMs": 2929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "— fits me rather neatly, doesn't it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733839,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it fits me staggeringly well, must\nhave been made to have me in it!\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739589,
    "dDurationMs": 5031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is such a powerful idea that as the sun\nrises in the sky and the air heats up and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744620,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as, gradually, the puddle gets smaller and\nsmaller, it's still frantically hanging on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748730,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the notion that everything's going to be\nalright, because this world was meant to have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753870,
    "dDurationMs": 6159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "him in it, was built to have him in it; so\nthe moment he disappears catches him rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760029,
    "dDurationMs": 1161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by surprise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761190,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think this may be something we need to be\non the watch out for."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766160,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Used well the anthropic principle gives us\na deep perspective on our place in the cosmos,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772420,
    "dDurationMs": 5419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and can also be a powerful, albeit slippery\nscientific tool."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777839,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But misused it can lead to unscientific conclusions\n- as can any tool in science."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783509,
    "dDurationMs": 6041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll come back to some of the wilder predictions\nof both good and bad anthropic reasoning real"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789550,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "soon, and talk about what you can know about\nyour universe, given your privileged status"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796060,
    "dDurationMs": 4699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a typical conscious observer of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800760,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we have a very special thank you to\nDr. Alex Flournoy of the Colorado School of Mines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807420,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr. Flournoy, several of your students wanted to thank you for unlocking the mysteries of physics for them and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813480,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to let you know their thoughts are with you\nby sponsoring this video at the Big Bang level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819810,
    "dDurationMs": 6029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now based on the very touching words\nthey sent to us you are clearly some kind"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825839,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a hero, who’s super power is to spread\ntheir vast knowledge of physics across the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830089,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multi-verse and unleash a Star Trek-esque\nscience utopia here on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835399,
    "dDurationMs": 6161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To speed that up, we've linked to your amazing\nlectures on particle physics and general relativity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841560,
    "dDurationMs": 1459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843019,
    "dDurationMs": 5401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So from your students and everyone here at\nSpace Time we wish you well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848420,
    "dDurationMs": 3859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we talked about how the constants\nof nature seem to be fine tuned for life in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852279,
    "dDurationMs": 5791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe - and how this may imply that\nthere are countless universes beyond our own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858070,
    "dDurationMs": 3189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Discussion in the comments was lively, to\nsay the least."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861259,
    "dDurationMs": 5221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many people had the following objection: they\nsay that the universe isn't really fine-tuned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866480,
    "dDurationMs": 5299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for life or for observers because there could\nbe many types of observer very different to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871780,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ourselves, that could potentially exist if\nthe fundamental constants were different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876660,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, actually, fine tuning arguments for the fundamental\nconstants for the most part take that into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882569,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "account."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883879,
    "dDurationMs": 6291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can probably assume that for an intelligent\nobserver to emerge in any universe, that universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890170,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must be capable of forming complex structures\n- whether or not it looks like life as we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895130,
    "dDurationMs": 1259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896389,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the universe needs to last a reasonable\namount of time, have stable regions and energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902959,
    "dDurationMs": 4221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sources for those structures to form, and\nhave some building blocks - whether or not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907180,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they look like atoms as we know them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909880,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Much of the parameter space that the constants\nof nature could have taken eliminate one or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914490,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more of these factors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916470,
    "dDurationMs": 5479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So while there may be many small parts of\nthat parameter space where observers can arise,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921949,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most of it - hence most universes - should\nbe devoid of observers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926780,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few of you also pointed out that I missed\none possibility - perhaps the dials defining"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932380,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fundamental constants were neither randomly\nset nor deliberately tuned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937900,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps there's some unknown physics prinicple\nthat demands they have exaclty the values"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943180,
    "dDurationMs": 1460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944640,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well right - that's possible - but the point is\nthat UNLESS that principle is somehow connected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950240,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the universe's later developing life\nand structure, why should it have landed on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955910,
    "dDurationMs": 3859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the rare combinations amenable to life\nand structure?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959769,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's just as easy to imagine a physical principle\nthat gives you only one universe with an unavoidable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966569,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combination of fundamental constants that\nwas completely devoid of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971459,
    "dDurationMs": 5201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So I still count this option as either \"getting\nlucky\" or that the later emergence of life"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976660,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was somehow retrocausal of the universe's\nknob-setting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Between these options and the multiverse,\nI think I prefer the multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986329,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AdlockHungry makes a great point: surely\nif this were the Goldilocks universe there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be life on almost every planetary body,\neven in this solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996280,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would certainly be true if such fertile\nuniverses where anywhere near as common as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001990,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our relatively barren one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003990,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The self-sampling assumption says we should\nassume we're a typical observer - so maybe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010180,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most typical observers are in relatively\nbarren universes, and there are just way more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015440,
    "dDurationMs": 2259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017699,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That may be telling us something about how\nthe constants of nature get set - it may be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021839,
    "dDurationMs": 6291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "improbable to get those constants fine-tuned\nfor life - but the more fine-tuned they are,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028130,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the more improbable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029350,
    "dDurationMs": 5839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Keith Strang correctly points out that we\nalso have to talk about fine tuning of parameters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035189,
    "dDurationMs": 3431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get a multiverse in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038620,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicists are struggling to make eternal\ninflation work without its own fine tuning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044490,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that may be just as bad as the fine tuning\nneeded for life in our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049150,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that's a challenge, but inflation is still\nnot so well understood, so it seems there's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054880,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plenty of opportunity to solve this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057050,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it can't be solved then we should ultimately\ndismiss the theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enotdetcelfer has an idea for a physics problem:\niterate through the settings of the constants"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066800,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of nature and figure out the map of possible\nsetting that support self-propagating pattern entities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073880,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, find all possible combinations\nof constants that can produce observers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078220,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that sounds like a great challenge question!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081440,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If anyone does this they get a free universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084720,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a t-shirt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086400,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although if you can do this you can probably\nalso build your own portal gun and visit all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090500,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universes you want."
    } ]
  } ]
}
